WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.255
Lesson 6.2.

00:00:02.255 --> 00:00:06.605
Tokenomics: supply, unlocks, FDV, and value capture.

00:00:06.605 --> 00:00:08.941
Today you'll learn to read a token's

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:11.971
own supply schedule, and judge whether protocol

00:00:11.971 --> 00:00:14.685
success genuinely reaches the token itself.

00:00:15.985 --> 00:00:18.480
Here's the objective, in one sentence.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.514
Read a token's supply schedule, and judge whether

00:00:21.514 --> 00:00:24.790
the protocol's own success actually reaches the token

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.763
you'd be holding, rather than simply assuming it

00:00:27.763 --> 00:00:30.615
does because the protocol itself is doing well.

00:00:31.915 --> 00:00:35.982
Here's the full picture this lesson builds: circulating and

00:00:35.982 --> 00:00:39.371
max supply, market cap and F.D.V., the unlock and

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:43.302
emissions schedule that grows supply over time, and value

00:00:43.302 --> 00:00:47.030
capture, whether protocol revenue actually reaches you.

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.932
Here's all four pieces, named individually, since each one

00:00:51.932 --> 00:00:55.895
answers a genuinely different question about the same token.

00:00:55.895 --> 00:00:59.850
Supply terms: how many tokens exist now, versus ever.

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.984
Valuation terms: what the market actually says it's

00:01:02.984 --> 00:01:05.393
worth, at each of those two counts.

00:01:05.393 --> 00:01:09.938
Supply growth: how fast new tokens actually enter circulation.

00:01:09.938 --> 00:01:12.424
And value capture: whether the protocol's

00:01:12.424 --> 00:01:14.378
own success actually reaches you.

00:01:15.678 --> 00:01:18.408
Circulating supply vs. max supply.

00:01:19.708 --> 00:01:23.142
Circulating supply is the count that actually matters

00:01:23.142 --> 00:01:26.449
for trading right now: every token that's genuinely

00:01:26.449 --> 00:01:29.293
tradeable today, out in the open market.

00:01:29.293 --> 00:01:32.757
Max supply, sometimes called total supply, is every

00:01:32.757 --> 00:01:36.088
token that could ever genuinely exist, once every

00:01:36.088 --> 00:01:39.353
future unlock and emission has actually happened.

00:01:40.653 --> 00:01:44.178
Worth being direct about why that gap actually matters.

00:01:44.178 --> 00:01:47.760
If circulating supply is small relative to max supply,

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:51.277
most of the token's eventual total hasn't reached the

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:54.404
market yet, and it's going to, on some schedule

00:01:54.404 --> 00:01:56.618
you can actually go find and read.

00:01:57.918 --> 00:02:00.093
Market cap vs. F.D.V.

00:02:01.393 --> 00:02:03.746
Two formulas, both built from the same

00:02:03.746 --> 00:02:07.003
token price, each answering a different question.

00:02:07.003 --> 00:02:10.555
Market cap: price, multiplied by circulating supply,

00:02:10.555 --> 00:02:13.938
the value of what's genuinely tradeable today.

00:02:13.938 --> 00:02:17.914
F.D.V., fully diluted valuation: price, multiplied by

00:02:17.914 --> 00:02:20.933
max supply, the value of everything that

00:02:20.933 --> 00:02:23.658
will ever exist, once fully released.

00:02:24.958 --> 00:02:27.353
Here's the source's own worked example.

00:02:27.353 --> 00:02:29.724
A token priced at two dollars, with a

00:02:29.724 --> 00:02:32.968
circulating supply of one hundred million: that's a

00:02:32.968 --> 00:02:35.888
market cap of two hundred million dollars.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:38.802
The same two-dollar price, applied to a max

00:02:38.802 --> 00:02:41.583
supply of one billion, gives an F.D.V. of

00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:44.696
two billion dollars, ten times larger than the

00:02:44.696 --> 00:02:48.073
market cap you'd actually be trading against today.

00:02:49.373 --> 00:02:52.443
Here's what that ten-times gap actually tells you:

00:02:52.443 --> 00:02:54.790
nine out of every ten tokens that will

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.618
ever exist haven't reached the market yet.

00:02:57.618 --> 00:03:01.212
A low market cap sitting underneath a huge F.D.V.

00:03:01.212 --> 00:03:06.172
isn't automatically disqualifying, but it's a real, specific warning

00:03:06.172 --> 00:03:09.550
worth investigating, not a detail to skip past.

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:11.939
Quick check.

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:14.241
A token trades at five dollars, with

00:03:14.241 --> 00:03:16.979
a circulating supply of twenty million, and

00:03:16.979 --> 00:03:19.519
a max supply of one hundred million.

00:03:19.519 --> 00:03:20.994
What's its F.D.V.?

00:03:25.414 --> 00:03:28.991
The answer: five hundred million dollars — five dollars,

00:03:28.991 --> 00:03:32.254
multiplied by the max supply of one hundred million.

00:03:33.554 --> 00:03:34.799
Unlocks and vesting.

00:03:36.099 --> 00:03:38.933
Unlocks, also called vesting, are team and

00:03:38.933 --> 00:03:42.030
investor tokens released into circulation on a

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:45.295
fixed schedule, agreed to well before launch.

00:03:45.295 --> 00:03:48.146
Every unlocked token becomes new supply that

00:03:48.146 --> 00:03:50.744
its recipient is genuinely free to sell,

00:03:50.744 --> 00:03:53.215
whether or not they actually choose to.

00:03:54.515 --> 00:03:58.070
Here's the source's own unlock example, made visual.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:01.933
Circulating supply starts at one hundred million tokens.

00:04:01.933 --> 00:04:05.547
One month later, a fifty-million-token unlock lands, and

00:04:05.547 --> 00:04:09.034
circulating supply jumps to one hundred fifty million:

00:04:09.034 --> 00:04:11.823
a fifty percent increase, in a single month.

00:04:13.123 --> 00:04:16.198
Here's why that fifty-percent jump matters so much.

00:04:16.198 --> 00:04:18.902
Unless real demand for the token actually

00:04:18.902 --> 00:04:21.027
grows to match it, that much new

00:04:21.027 --> 00:04:23.731
sellable supply, arriving all at once, is

00:04:23.731 --> 00:04:26.928
genuine, heavy selling pressure on the price.

00:04:26.928 --> 00:04:28.955
This is exactly the kind of event

00:04:28.955 --> 00:04:31.816
worth knowing about in advance, not discovering

00:04:31.816 --> 00:04:33.843
after the price has already moved.

00:04:35.143 --> 00:04:37.449
Here's the actual fork in the road,

00:04:37.449 --> 00:04:41.145
once that fifty-million-token unlock genuinely lands.

00:04:41.145 --> 00:04:43.392
If real demand has grown enough to

00:04:43.392 --> 00:04:45.831
absorb it, price holds, and the extra

00:04:45.831 --> 00:04:48.827
supply simply gets absorbed by new buyers.

00:04:48.827 --> 00:04:52.046
If demand hasn't grown to match, recipients selling

00:04:52.046 --> 00:04:54.770
into a thin market can genuinely push price

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.432
down, sometimes sharply, in a short window.

00:04:58.732 --> 00:05:01.242
Worth knowing the actual shape most real

00:05:01.242 --> 00:05:03.998
vesting schedules take, since it changes how

00:05:03.998 --> 00:05:06.257
urgently a given unlock matters.

00:05:06.257 --> 00:05:09.546
Many token launches use a one-year cliff: genuinely

00:05:09.546 --> 00:05:12.583
zero tokens unlock for the first year, followed

00:05:12.583 --> 00:05:15.492
by linear monthly unlocks spread over the two

00:05:15.492 --> 00:05:17.437
or three years after that.

00:05:17.437 --> 00:05:20.054
A schedule still inside its cliff period

00:05:20.054 --> 00:05:22.734
carries a very different urgency than one

00:05:22.734 --> 00:05:24.840
already deep into linear unlocks.

00:05:26.139 --> 00:05:26.944
Emissions.

00:05:28.244 --> 00:05:32.252
Emissions are ongoing, newly minted tokens, handed out as

00:05:32.252 --> 00:05:36.284
rewards, most often to liquidity providers or stakers.

00:05:36.284 --> 00:05:40.338
Unlike an unlock, which releases tokens that already existed,

00:05:40.338 --> 00:05:45.046
an emission genuinely creates brand-new supply, diluting every existing

00:05:45.046 --> 00:05:48.054
holder a little, every single time it happens.

00:05:49.354 --> 00:05:51.421
Worth connecting this directly back

00:05:51.421 --> 00:05:53.559
to last lesson's cash-flow step.

00:05:53.559 --> 00:05:56.837
If a protocol's own real revenue isn't growing fast

00:05:56.837 --> 00:06:00.429
enough to offset its ongoing emissions, the token supply

00:06:00.429 --> 00:06:03.896
keeps expanding faster than genuine demand for it, and

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:07.489
price pressure is the structural result, not an accident.

00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:09.799
Value capture.

00:06:11.099 --> 00:06:13.585
Here's the real difference value capture

00:06:13.585 --> 00:06:15.644
actually makes, side by side.

00:06:15.644 --> 00:06:20.211
With genuine value capture, real, specific mechanisms send protocol

00:06:20.211 --> 00:06:23.703
revenue to holders: a direct fee share, or enforced

00:06:23.703 --> 00:06:27.630
token buybacks, written into the contracts themselves.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.594
Without it, all of that same revenue stays

00:06:30.594 --> 00:06:33.835
entirely with the team or the D.A.O. treasury,

00:06:33.835 --> 00:06:37.075
and holders benefit only indirectly, if at all.

00:06:38.375 --> 00:06:42.090
Here's the actual test worth applying, every single time.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:45.268
Don't just ask whether a protocol is profitable;

00:06:45.268 --> 00:06:48.380
ask specifically how that profit reaches you as

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:51.363
a token holder, and whether that mechanism is

00:06:51.363 --> 00:06:55.124
genuinely enforced on-chain, or merely a stated intention

00:06:55.124 --> 00:06:56.940
the team could change later.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.804
Here's a specific, real pattern worth being able

00:07:01.804 --> 00:07:04.931
to spot on sight: a low circulating market

00:07:04.931 --> 00:07:08.204
cap sitting under a genuinely huge F.D.V. On

00:07:08.204 --> 00:07:11.995
its own, that's not automatically disqualifying.

00:07:11.995 --> 00:07:15.132
But combined with heavy near-term unlocks and no

00:07:15.132 --> 00:07:18.845
real value-capture mechanism, it's a pattern that's shown

00:07:18.845 --> 00:07:21.598
up in plenty of real token launches, worth

00:07:21.598 --> 00:07:25.055
naming precisely rather than reacting to only vaguely.

00:07:26.355 --> 00:07:27.445
Quick check.

00:07:27.445 --> 00:07:29.880
Why do unlocks matter for a token's price?

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:36.930
The answer: they add real, sellable supply

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:39.622
that recipients may choose to sell, whether

00:07:39.622 --> 00:07:41.090
or not they actually do.

00:07:42.390 --> 00:07:44.310
Reading a real supply schedule.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.621
Here's the actual sequence, run on

00:07:47.621 --> 00:07:49.875
any real token you're considering.

00:07:49.875 --> 00:07:52.220
Look up circulating and max supply.

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.821
Calculate both market cap and F.D.V.,

00:07:54.821 --> 00:07:57.380
and compare the gap between them.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:08:00.275
Find the next twelve months of scheduled unlocks.

00:08:00.275 --> 00:08:02.730
Check the current emissions rate, and whether

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.005
real revenue is growing to offset it.

00:08:05.005 --> 00:08:08.204
And identify the value-capture mechanism, or honestly

00:08:08.204 --> 00:08:10.100
write down that there isn't one.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:14.528
This is exactly the same discipline last lesson's

00:08:14.528 --> 00:08:18.031
evidenced-not-assumed rule asked for, applied here to a

00:08:18.031 --> 00:08:21.897
token's own numbers instead of a protocol's dependencies.

00:08:21.897 --> 00:08:23.800
Every one of these five checks

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:26.432
has a real, specific, findable answer.

00:08:26.432 --> 00:08:28.467
None of them are a matter of opinion.

00:08:29.767 --> 00:08:32.388
Three more questions worth asking directly,

00:08:32.388 --> 00:08:34.892
if a schedule genuinely isn't public.

00:08:34.892 --> 00:08:37.845
Is the full unlock schedule actually published

00:08:37.845 --> 00:08:40.972
anywhere, or only described in general terms.

00:08:40.972 --> 00:08:44.118
Has that schedule ever genuinely been changed after

00:08:44.118 --> 00:08:46.717
launch, and if so, in which direction.

00:08:46.717 --> 00:08:49.768
And is there any lockup-extension option that

00:08:49.768 --> 00:08:52.621
team or investor tokens could actually use

00:08:52.621 --> 00:08:54.412
to delay their own unlocks.

00:08:55.712 --> 00:08:56.807
Before you move on.

00:08:58.107 --> 00:09:01.267
Confirm three things before calling this lesson done.

00:09:01.267 --> 00:09:05.330
Market cap has actually been compared against F.D.V., not

00:09:05.330 --> 00:09:08.642
just one of the two looked up in isolation.

00:09:08.642 --> 00:09:10.460
The next twelve months of unlocks

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.417
have genuinely been listed out.

00:09:12.417 --> 00:09:15.748
And a value-capture mechanism has been identified, or

00:09:15.748 --> 00:09:19.017
honestly marked as none, rather than left unanswered.

00:09:20.317 --> 00:09:21.357
One more.

00:09:21.357 --> 00:09:23.612
What is value capture, in one sentence?

00:09:28.032 --> 00:09:31.191
The answer: an enforceable way that a protocol's

00:09:31.191 --> 00:09:34.222
own success actually benefits the token itself.

00:09:35.522 --> 00:09:37.727
Let's recap this entire lesson.

00:09:37.727 --> 00:09:40.662
Circulating supply is what trades today; max

00:09:40.662 --> 00:09:43.702
supply is everything that will ever exist.

00:09:43.702 --> 00:09:46.579
Market cap and F.D.V. use that same price

00:09:46.579 --> 00:09:49.799
against those two very different counts, and a

00:09:49.799 --> 00:09:52.907
large gap between them is a real warning.

00:09:52.907 --> 00:09:55.600
Unlocks and emissions both grow supply, on

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.217
a schedule you can actually go check.

00:09:58.217 --> 00:10:01.343
And value capture asks whether the protocol's own

00:10:01.343 --> 00:10:05.157
success genuinely reaches you, enforced, not merely promised.

00:10:06.457 --> 00:10:08.985
Pick one real token, and run all five

00:10:08.985 --> 00:10:11.947
checks on its own supply schedule today.

00:10:11.947 --> 00:10:16.092
Next up, Lesson 6.3: governance and DAOs.
